---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **파는 자리.** (`/for-industry`) 2번 지시 12:2x.
 *
 * ── 왜 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * 살 만한 곳을 208곳까지 이름째 찾았고 여정표를 A등급 17곳 전부 만들어 뒀다.
 * ⛔ 그런데 **그들이 올 자리가 없었다.** 편지를 보내도 「어디를 보라」 할 데가 없다.
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **없는 것을 팔지 않는다.** 여기 적힌 셋은 오늘 건넬 수 있는 것뿐이다.
 * ⛔ **값을 안 적는다.** 업계 관행이 비공개이고, 우리 A/B/C 연 구독값은 아직 **가설**이다.
 *    찾은 수(하한선·첫걸음)만 있고 상한이 없다 — 화면에 값을 박으면 못 찾은 것을 찾은 척하게 된다.
 * ⛔ **못 하는 것을 같은 크기로 적는다.** 파는 자리라고 한계를 작게 쓰지 않는다.
 * ⛔ **줄세우지 않는다.** 회사를 크기 순으로 안 놓는다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 자료 파일에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import titles from '../../data/wikitip-title-pages.json';
import markets from '../../data/wikitip-markets.json';
import firms from '../../data/wikitip-catalogue-reach.json';
import world from '../../data/wikitip-world-share.json';
import firmPages from '../../data/wikitip-firm-pages.json';

const MAIL = 'cs@klifedesign.net';
const 편지 = (s: string) => `mailto:${MAIL}?subject=${encodeURIComponent(s)}`;

const TITLE = 'For rights holders, distributors and broadcasters';
const DESC =
  `Netflix publishes global hours to its suppliers. It does not publish which country. We hold `
  + `${world.weekCount} weeks of country-level top 10 data across ${markets.countryCount} markets — `
  + `${titles.titleCount} Korean titles and ${firms.firmsTotal} companies — and can tell you where a `
  + `catalogue travelled, market by market.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Korean title travel data',
  description: DESC,
  provider: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  areaServed: 'Worldwide',
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/for-industry">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Netflix gives its suppliers <b>global hours</b>. It does not give them <b>which country</b>. If
    you hold rights to a Korean title, that is the column missing from your own reporting — and it is
    the one that decides where the next title opens.
  </p>
  <p class="q">
    We have been counting that column, publicly, since {world.weekFrom}.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>What we hold</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Weekly country top 10 lists</td>
            <td class="tot">{world.weekCount} weeks</td>
            <td>{world.weekFrom} to {world.weekTo}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Markets</td>
            <td class="tot">{markets.countryCount}</td>
            <td>Russia excluded — Netflix withdrew from it</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Chart places counted</td>
            <td class="tot">{world.totalSlots.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td>of which {world.koreanSlots.toLocaleString('en-US')} are Korean titles</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Korean titles that charted</td>
            <td class="tot">{titles.titleCount}</td>
            <td>{titles.pageCount} have a page of their own</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Companies credited on them</td>
            <td class="tot">{firms.firmsTotal}</td>
            <td>from Wikidata production, broadcaster and distributor credits</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      All of it is measured from Netflix's own published lists and from Wikidata. Nothing here is
      licensed from a panel or estimated from a model, which is also why there are things it cannot
      tell you — those are listed further down, at the same size.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Three things we can hand over</h2>

    <h3>1. Your titles' journey — one sheet per company</h3>
    <p class="body">
      Every market each of your titles charted in, with places held, best position reached, the first
      and last week it appeared there, and which single market took the most of it. One page, your
      catalogue only.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Ready today for {firms.grades[0].firms} companies</b> — the ones whose catalogue we can see
      most completely. For any of the other {firms.firmsTotal - firms.grades[0].firms} we say up front
      how much of your catalogue we hold before you decide, because a sheet built on half your titles
      is worth less than knowing it is half.
    </p>

    <h3>2. The market panel — {markets.countryCount} countries side by side</h3>
    <p class="body">
      For each market: how many chart places Korean titles took, how many distinct titles that was,
      how few titles fill half of it, and how that has moved year by year. This is the table you want
      before deciding where a title opens, not after.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The public version is at <a href="/catalogue-depth">catalogue depth</a>. The deliverable is the
      same figures as a file, with the column dictionary and the exclusions written down.
    </p>

    <h3>3. The title panel — {titles.titleCount} Korean titles, market by market</h3>
    <p class="body">
      One row per title per market: places, peak position, first and last week. The published pages
      carry {titles.rowsTotalPublished.toLocaleString('en-US')} such rows; the file carries the rest,
      including the titles too thin to be worth a page.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      A sample is public at <a href="/titles">every Korean title that has charted</a> — open any of
      them and you are looking at the same data, one title at a time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Questions this answers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Where did our last title actually travel, and where did it stop?</li>
      <li>Which markets take Korean series but not Korean films, or the other way round?</li>
      <li>Is a market's Korean chart shared among many titles, or filled by two or three?</li>
      <li>Did a title that stopped charting come back later — and did anything else of ours come back with it?</li>
      <li>How does our catalogue's reach compare with the median charting Korean catalogue?</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      Each of those has a public page behind it. If your question is not on this list, ask it — the
      useful answer is often that our tables cannot reach it, and we would rather say so.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What this data cannot tell you</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      A top 10 is a <b>rank list, not a viewing count</b>. A title outside a country's top 10 is
      invisible here at any level of viewing, and two titles at number 4 in different markets are not
      the same number of people. Netflix publishes hours only for its global lists, so we cannot weight
      a market by size.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      We also cannot tell you <b>why</b> anything moved. Promotion, release timing, dubbing, a cast
      member in the news and a public holiday all look identical in a rank table. Anyone selling you a
      reason from this data is selling you something the data does not contain.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      And company credits come from Wikidata, which is incomplete: a credit records that a company is
      attached, not how much of the work it held, and a company whose titles Wikidata does not record
      is absent rather than small. We publish that coverage figure beside every company-level result.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/*
    🔴 2026-08-21 — **파는 자리인데 값이 없었다.** 사장님: 「시장조사해서 결정해」.
      쟀다(2026-08-21) —
        Chartmetric  무료 / $10 / 약 $60 / 약 $150   **달마다**
        Soundcharts  $10 / $49 / $129               **달마다**
        Datarade     장터 자체가 $50~$250/달. 낱개 자료는 일회 구매도 있으나 값이 공개 안 됨
        회사 안       6번 서울마켓 $29 one-time (2번 실측)
      ⚠ 곧바로 견줄 **일회 구매 자료값**은 공개된 것을 못 찾았다. 위 넷은 대부분 **구독**이다.
      ⭐ 그래서 구독을 안 판다. 우리 것은 **날짜가 박힌 완성된 표**다 — 한 번 사서 갖는 물건.
      ⛔ Chartmetric·Soundcharts 는 스트리밍을 본다. 동남아 네 판 **읽힘**은 아무도 안 판다.
        그래서 「싼 대체품」이 아니라 **다른 물건**으로 값 매긴다.
      정한 값 — 낱개 $79 · 둘 묶음 $129 · 주문 조사 $490
        $79 는 Soundcharts Pro 한 달($129)보다 낮고 6번 $29 보다 높다. 패널이 더 크고 일회다.
  */}
  <section id="price">
    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>What you get</th><th>Size</th><th>Price</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">People panel — actors and music acts, four Southeast Asian Wikipedias, twelve months</td>
            <td class="fine">1,023 actors · 1,701 music acts</td>
            <td><b>$79</b> one-time</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">Places panel — Korean places read abroad, same four editions, twelve months</td>
            <td class="fine">2,219 places</td>
            <td><b>$79</b> one-time</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">Both panels together</td>
            <td class="fine">one delivery, one dictionary</td>
            <td><b>$129</b> one-time</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">Commissioned study — your artists or titles, written up in one sheet</td>
            <td class="fine">up to 20 names, four countries</td>
            <td><b>$490</b></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>One-time, not a subscription.</b> Each panel is a dated table: a twelve-month window that
      does not change after you buy it. You are not renting access to a dashboard, and there is
      nothing to cancel. CSV and JSON, with a column dictionary that says what every field means and
      what it cannot mean.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Why this number.</b> The tools this sits beside are subscriptions — Chartmetric runs about
      $60 to $150 a month and Soundcharts $49 to $129 a month, and both of them read streaming.
      None of them measure who is being <em>read about</em> in Indonesian, Vietnamese, Thai and
      Malay. We priced a finished panel below one month of those tools, because it is a different
      thing and should not pretend to replace them.
    </p>
    <p class="warn">
      <b>There is no checkout on this page.</b> You email, we answer within one working day, and we
      invoice. If the tables do not cover your question we say so and do not sell you the near miss.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to ask</h2>
    <p class="body">
      <b>Email <a href={편지('K Culture Wire — data enquiry')}>{MAIL}</a></b> and say what you are
      trying to decide. Naming the company helps, because the first thing we will tell you is how much
      of your catalogue we can actually see.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>What happens then.</b> We reply within one working day, in Korean or English, with a plain
      answer to whether these tables cover your question — including when they do not, which is the
      more useful reply. If they do, you get the file and the column dictionary. If they do not, we
      say what would have to exist, and we do not sell you the near miss.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Before you write, read something we published.</b> Every figure on this page has a table
      behind it: <a href="/catalogue-depth">the markets</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/catalogue-reach">how far a catalogue travels</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/two-pipelines">broadcast and streaming as two pipelines</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/returns">titles that came back</a>. If those look wrong to you, say so in the same
      email — that is a better first conversation than a price.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/*
    🔴 2026-08-09 21:5x — 회사 지면 여덟 장을 냈는데 **여기서 가는 길이 없으면 없는 것과 같다.**
      ⛔ 파는 자리(/for-industry)가 미리보기로 못 데려가면 손님이 물건을 못 본다.
  */}
  <section>
    <h2>See it before you ask for it</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">
      Eight companies have a public page showing how far their titles travelled — markets reached,
      how many held a title in the same week, and where the places fell. The sheet goes further; the
      page is enough to see whether the measurement is the one you need.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Company</th><th>Titles that charted</th><th>Markets</th><th>Most at once</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {firmPages.firms.map((x) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm"><a href={`/firm/${x.slug}`}>{x.firm}</a></td>
              <td>{x.titlesThatCharted}</td>
              <td>{x.markets}</td>
              <td class="tot">{x.widestWeekCountries}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note fine">
      {firmPages.whyTheseEight} If your company is not among them, the same sheet can be built from
      the same data — <a href="/contact">tell us the name</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Who we are</h2>
    <p class="note">
      K Culture Wire counts what Netflix and Wikidata publish about Korean film and television, names
      every source, and says what it left out. Not affiliated with any agency, label or studio.
      {' '}<a href="/about">How we count</a> · <a href="/corrections">Every figure we have had to
      change</a> · <a href="/contact">Contact</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
